.TH "anything\-sync\-daemon" "1" "" ""
.SH NAME
\f[CR]anything\-sync\-daemon\f[R] \- Symlinks and syncs user specified
dirs to RAM thus reducing HDD/SSD calls and speeding\-up the system.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]anything\-sync\-daemon\f[R] (\f[CR]asd\f[R]) is a tiny
pseudo\-daemon designed to manage user specified directories referred to
as sync targets from here on out, in tmpfs and to periodically sync them
back to the physical disk (HDD/SSD).
This is accomplished via a bind mounting step and an innovative use of
rsync to maintain synchronization between a tmpfs copy and media\-bound
backups.
Additionally, asd features several crash\-recovery features.
.PP
Design goals of asd:
.IP \(bu 2
Completely transparent user experience.
.IP \(bu 2
Reduced wear to physical disks (particularly SSDs).
.IP \(bu 2
Speed.
.PP
Since the sync targets are relocated into tmpfs (RAM disk), the
corresponding onslaught of I/O associated with system usage of them is
also redirected from the physical disk to RAM, thus reducing wear to the
physical disk and also improving speed and responsiveness.
.SH SETUP
\f[CR]/etc/asd.conf\f[R] contains all user managed settings.
Optionally another file can be used by setting the \f[CR]ASDCONF\f[R]
environment variable.
.PP
\f[B]NOTE:\f[R] edits made to \f[CR]/etc/asd.conf\f[R] while asd is
running will be applied only after asd has been restarted from the init
service.
.IP \(bu 2
At a minimum, define the sync targets to be managed by asd in the
\f[CR]WHATTOSYNC\f[R] array.
Syntax below.
.IP \(bu 2
Optionally uncomment and define the location of your distro\(cqs tmpfs*
in the \f[CR]VOLATILE\f[R] variable.
.IP \(bu 2
Optionally enable the use of overlayfs to improve sync speed even
further and use a smaller memory footprint.
Do this in the \f[CR]USE_OVERLAYFS\f[R] variable.
Note that this option requires your kernel to be configured to use
either the `overlay' or `overlayfs' module.
See the FAQ below for additional details on this feature.
.IP \(bu 2
Optionally disable the use of crash\-recovery snapshots.
Do this in the \f[CR]USE_BACKUPS\f[R] variable.
.IP \(bu 2
Optionally define the number of crash\-recovery snapshots to keep.
Do this in the \f[CR]BACKUP_LIMIT\f[R] variable.
.PP
*Note that the default value of \(lq/tmp\(rq should work just fine for
the VOLATILE setting.
If using bleachbit, do NOT invoke it with the
\f[CR]\-\-clean system.tmp\f[R] switch or you will remove a key dot file
(.foo) from /tmp that asd needs to keep track of sync status.
Also note that using a value of \(lq/dev/shm\(rq can cause problems with
systemd\(cqs NAMESPACE spawning only when users enable the overlayfs
option.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
WHATTOSYNC=(\(aq/var/lib/monitorix\(aq \(aq/srv/http\(aq \(aq/foo/bar\(aq)

\f[I]# or\f[R]

WHATTOSYNC=(
\(aq/var/lib/monitorix\(aq
\(aq/srv/http\(aq
\(aq/foo/bar\(aq
)
.EE
.SH RUNNING ASD
.SS PREVIEW MODE
The preview option can be called to show users exactly what asd will
do/is doing based on the entries in \f[CR]/etc/asd.conf\f[R] as well
printout useful information such as dir size, paths, and if any recovery
snapshots have been created.
.IP
.EX
$ asd p

